## Notable design decisions

1. **Re-export hub, NOT physical relocation**. Every existing
   `pub(crate) const` stays in its source-of-truth file. The tuning
   module is a `pub use` aggregator. This kept the diff small + made
   the BYTE-IDENTICAL guarantee structurally obvious.

2. **`pub(crate) use` re-exports** for everything except the 2 NEW
   shared-value aliases. This avoids broadening the public API surface
   (per CLAUDE.md "DO NOT change the public API without approval").
   Sweep runners that live in the same crate (as binaries / examples)
   can read every tunable; external consumers cannot.

3. **Compile-time alias drift assertions**. Both
   `SMART_ZENJXL_PHOTO_MASK_P25_MIN` (4-site) and
   `SCREENSHOT_MEDIAN_THRESHOLD` (4-site) carry
   `const _: () = assert!(...)` so a future drift on any constituent
   site (e.g. someone changing `W44_166_VARIANT_Z_PHOTO_MASK_P25_MIN`
   from 85.0 to 90.0 alone) will fail to compile, not silently diverge.

4. **`#[allow(unused_imports)]` per submodule**. Production code reads
   each const through its source-of-truth path; the re-exports look
   unused unless a sweep-runner binary consumes them. Allowed
   per-submodule (not crate-wide) so only the intentional re-exports
   are silenced.

5. **`tuning-override` feature opts in std-only**. The runtime override
   layer uses `std::sync::OnceLock` (Rust 1.70+) for single-shot
   global install. Production builds without the feature pay zero cost
   — the runtime module compiles to nothing. Postcard is the wire
   format because the future sweep runner will emit binary configs;
   serde with `default` per-field tolerates additive struct evolution.

## DO NOT (binding for future agents)

- DO NOT add new `pub const` to `src/tuning.rs` that shadow originals.
  Use `pub(crate) use crate::vardct::<file>::CONST_NAME;`. The only
  exception is the existing 2 shared aliases, both with compile-time
  drift assertions.
- DO NOT remove the compile-time alias assertions. They are the only
  safeguard against silent drift of the 4-site shared values.
- DO NOT plumb `RuntimeTuning` reads into production code paths.
  Consumers read the source const directly; the override layer is for
  the sweep runner binary ONLY. Mixing them in production code would
  defeat the const-fold-friendly design.
- DO NOT touch `quant_weights` or `ac_strategy::K_BIAS` / `K_POW_*`
  values without decoder agreement — these are libjxl-spec values.
- DO NOT change the values in submodules. They are re-exports; the
  source site owns the value. Any edit happens in the source-of-truth
  file (and triggers the W44-194 divergence-table maintenance rule).

## Follow-ons (NOT in W44-211 scope)

1. **Expand `RuntimeTuning` field set**. The current scaffold carries
   only 6 fields (the highest-EV bisect targets). Future sweep-runner
   chunks will extend additively as new sweeps land.
2. **Move 4-site duplicates to single source**. The alias drift
   assertions catch divergence but the 4 constituent sites still
   exist. A future chunk could collapse them to a single source +
   `pub(crate) use` re-exports under the old names for back-compat.
   Not done here because each constituent has independent
   `docs/LIBJXL_DIVERGENCES.md` provenance + commit refs; collapsing
   prematurely would lose that history.
3. **Sweep-runner binary**. Live in `jxl-encoder/` as a `[[bin]]` or
   sibling `tuning-sweep-bin/` crate. Reads postcard config, calls
   `install()`, runs the standard encode pipeline, reports
   bytes/SSIM2/butteraugli/wall.
